{
 "audio": "",
 "voice": "ESTIMATED (no voice yet)",
 "estimated": true,
 "duration": 93.49,
 "sentences": [
  {
   "n": 1,
   "text": "The gaps between your fingers were made by cells that killed themselves.",
   "start": 0.35,
   "end": 4.925,
   "firstWord": 0
  },
  {
   "n": 2,
   "text": "Before you were born, your hand looked like a paddle.",
   "start": 5.47,
   "end": 9.095,
   "firstWord": 12
  },
  {
   "n": 3,
   "text": "Your fingers were joined by a thin web of tissue.",
   "start": 9.64,
   "end": 12.895,
   "firstWord": 22
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "text": "Then the cells in that web got a signal.",
   "start": 13.44,
   "end": 16.16,
   "firstWord": 32
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "text": "And they shut themselves down. On purpose.",
   "start": 16.705,
   "end": 19.645,
   "firstWord": 41
  },
  {
   "n": 6,
   "text": "This is called apoptosis. Programmed cell death.",
   "start": 20.19,
   "end": 23.43,
   "firstWord": 48
  },
  {
   "n": 7,
   "text": "Here's how a cell does it.",
   "start": 23.975,
   "end": 25.69,
   "firstWord": 55
  },
  {
   "n": 8,
   "text": "Inside are mitochondria, the parts that make the cell's energy.",
   "start": 26.235,
   "end": 30.31,
   "firstWord": 61
  },
  {
   "n": 9,
   "text": "When the signal comes, they release a protein called cytochrome c.",
   "start": 30.855,
   "end": 35.215,
   "firstWord": 71
  },
  {
   "n": 10,
   "text": "That switches on enzymes called caspases.",
   "start": 35.76,
   "end": 38.275,
   "firstWord": 82
  },
  {
   "n": 11,
   "text": "Caspases work like scissors. They cut the cell apart from the inside.",
   "start": 38.82,
   "end": 43.535,
   "firstWord": 88
  },
  {
   "n": 12,
   "text": "The cell shrinks. Its DNA gets chopped into pieces.",
   "start": 44.08,
   "end": 47.64,
   "firstWord": 100
  },
  {
   "n": 13,
   "text": "Then it breaks into small, neat packages, each wrapped in membrane.",
   "start": 48.185,
   "end": 52.765,
   "firstWord": 109
  },
  {
   "n": 14,
   "text": "The name comes from a Greek word for falling off, like leaves from a tree.",
   "start": 53.31,
   "end": 58.41,
   "firstWord": 120
  },
  {
   "n": 15,
   "text": "Nothing spills out, so there's no inflammation. No mess.",
   "start": 58.955,
   "end": 62.885,
   "firstWord": 135
  },
  {
   "n": 16,
   "text": "A cleaner cell called a macrophage comes along and eats the pieces.",
   "start": 63.43,
   "end": 67.755,
   "firstWord": 144
  },
  {
   "n": 17,
   "text": "And it's not just your fingers.",
   "start": 68.3,
   "end": 70.265,
   "firstWord": 156
  },
  {
   "n": 18,
   "text": "Your body uses this to remove cells that are damaged, infected, or no longer needed.",
   "start": 70.81,
   "end": 76.58,
   "firstWord": 162
  },
  {
   "n": 19,
   "text": "Billions of your cells do it every day.",
   "start": 77.125,
   "end": 79.71,
   "firstWord": 177
  },
  {
   "n": 20,
   "text": "When it fails, damaged cells can survive and keep dividing. That's one of the ways cancer starts.",
   "start": 80.255,
   "end": 86.915,
   "firstWord": 185
  },
  {
   "n": 21,
   "text": "So every time you spread your fingers, you're looking at cells that knew when to leave.",
   "start": 87.46,
   "end": 93.245,
   "firstWord": 202
  }
 ],
